From 9a112717e96a7afc06ac5dca87638e805f580d8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arch Contributor <contributor@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:14:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Fix build (linux-headers ship no sources) and add the MS OS
 0xEE handshake

prepare() looked for xpad.c under /usr/lib/modules/*/build/drivers/input/
joystick/, but Arch's linux-headers ships headers only - there is no driver
source in that tree (78 .c files vs 7493 .h). The build therefore always died
with "xpad.c not found in any kernel headers", and the scriptlet then hid that
by swallowing every dkms exit status. Fetch xpad.c from the matching kernel tag
instead, and stop ignoring failures.

Functionally, the GIP init packets 1.0 shipped are not sufficient. Measured on
a wired BTP-KP20D (20bc:5158): with the module loaded, xpad binds and the
packets are sent, yet the firmware still power-cycles into Switch emulation
(057e:2009) about 2s later. What it is actually waiting for is the Microsoft OS
descriptor request at index 0xEE that Windows issues while discovering the
"XUSB10" compatible ID. Hardware result:

  raw=0    vendor bRequest=0xEE, wValue=0, wIndex=4   <- works, device stays
  str=-11  standard GET_DESCRIPTOR(STRING, 0xEE)      <- firmware times out

Send the raw form first (the decision window is only ~2s) and keep the standard
form as a fallback for other KP models. The handshake now also runs from
probe(), not only when userspace opens the input device.

Other changes:
  * real checksums instead of SKIP; SPDX license identifier
  * drop LLVM=1 - it forces clang/lld, which are not in makedepends
  * stop shipping xpad.ko inside the package; DKMS owns that path
  * surface dkms failures instead of silencing them
  * stop duplicating the 0x5134/0x514a device table entries

Verified: the patched xpad.c is byte-identical to the hardware-tested tree, and
makepkg completes cleanly on 7.2.8-arch1-2.
